  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 238 PM CDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA deep trough is in place across the Great Basin, with \nsoutheast flow building across the central High Plains. Our CWA \nis still under the influence of westerly flow aloft, with an \nembedded shortwave in SW flow to the south of the region. \nSurface ridging is in place and drier north and eventually \nnortheast BL flow is undercutting the northern edge of this \ninitial precipitation and while a few sprinkles may sneak into \nthe south tonight, most locations are expected to remain dry \nthrough midday Monday. After the more organized system moves \nthrough the region Monday afternoon through Tuesday, southwest \nflow develops and persists at least through the end of the week \nand potentially beyond the weekend (some variance in clusters \nbetween ridging and SW by this weekend). The general trend \nshould be for seasonal temperatures to warm slightly from \nseasonal highs in the 40s/lower 50s to slightly above average \nhighs in the mid 50s/near 60. SW flow aloft inherently brings \nthe potential for additional chances for precipitation, however \ndue to the weak nature of the upper flow in the Plains and \nvariance in guidance resolving any embedded waves confidence in \nmeasurable precipitation/impacts is low beyond Tuesday. \n\n\n...Rain Monday into Tuesday...\n\nA stronger mid level wave organizes into a hybrid type system \nMonday with large scale ascent increasing across our CWA south \nto north late Monday afternoon through Tuesday. Initially drier \nair in northeast BL flow may delay measurable rainfall, however \nthe surge of deeper moisture with WAA and at least some 700MB \ndeformation banding will help saturate the low levels and \nperiods of moderate (locally heavy) rainfall would be expected. \nAs is usually the case with dominant WAA patterns, stronger \nareas of frontogenesis will tend to be more transient making \ncoverage of higher totals 1\"+ harder to anticipate. Better \ncoverage of rain will tend to initially be along and south of US\nHighway 2, though the track of the mid/upper low will \neventually allow rain to overspread northwest/north central MN. \nHigh 24hr probabilities (60-80%) for 0.25\" or more rainfall are \nshown by NBM across southeast ND and west central MN, with a \n30-50% chance in similar areas for greater than 1\" of rainfall.\n\nRegarding snowfall: As with previous rounds of guidance the \ntemperature profiles may at times be cold enough to support a \nmix or change over to snow, particularly in higher terrain areas\nwest of the Red River Valley late Monday night/early Tuesday \nmorning. However, as HREF/REFS/NBM all show a very weak signal, \nwith lighter rates the chance for any snow to accumulate will be\ndiminished due to warmer ground temperatures. Still, a few \nnovelty snowflakes or a light dusting on grassy/elevated \nsurfaces may occur (best chance west of the Red River Valley (no\nimpacts).",
